          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "preparation",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Research task-parallel quicksort for the 4-core ARM64 target. Preserve the generated array and final MD5 computation; parallelize disjoint partitions with OpenMP tasks, using a tunable size cutoff and task-local or otherwise race-free pivot selection. Measure task overhead and scaling, and verify the exact output on all correctness cases.",
            "B": "Independently investigate a generic parallel merge-sort design: sort disjoint chunks in parallel, then merge them in parallel using bounded scratch storage. Handle duplicates and arbitrary integer ordering correctly. Compare end-to-end runtime, including allocation and merge costs, against the baseline rather than assuming parallelism helps at every size.",
            "C": "Investigate a robust serial quicksort improvement as a separate candidate: reduce recursion overhead, use insertion sort for small partitions, and consider an introspective fallback to avoid pathological quadratic behavior. Measure whether these changes help this workload; preserve all generated values and the checksum semantics.",
            "G": "Integrate only candidates with correctness evidence and measured gains. Benchmark each candidate and the combined program end-to-end against the valid baseline (mean 0.068951954 s; correctness passed 4 cases) on the specified 4-core ARM64 target. Combinations may regress relative to their inputs, so measure them rather than adding individual gains; retain the best correct result and report tradeoffs. Do not alter the workload, oracle, compiler, budgets, or target, and do not exploit expected answers or test-specific values.",
            "analysis": "The provided source generates the array, sorts it, checks ordering, and hashes the sorted integer bytes; correctness therefore requires a genuine sort and the same generation/checksum behavior. The baseline is valid, with mean time 0.068951954 seconds and four correctness cases passed. With four CPU cores and no GPU, the most grounded parallel experiments are task quicksort and chunked parallel merge sort, while a serial robustness/tuning path offers an independent comparison. Shared global pivot RNG state must not become a data race."
