          "plan": {
            "A": "Investigate memoizing the recursive cost by the first-matching node index, preferably using the fast ID lookup from B. Cache each selected path suffix so repeated queries or shared DAG suffixes are not recomputed. Preserve the source’s exact neighbor choice: maximize the neighbor’s node value (not its computed path cost), start the maximum at zero, and keep the first neighbor on ties. The ID sidecar alone measured 9.276 ms, so do not pursue it as a standalone optimization; benchmark the memoized version end to end and verify correctness.",
            "B": "Use ID lookup as the primary research direction: the two hash-index variants are the fastest observed at 5.660 and 5.679 ms versus the correct baseline’s 11.609 ms. Explore modest, measured alternatives such as bucket layout, load factor, hash/probe strategy, and scalar versus grouped probing on the ARM64 target. Preserve first-insertion behavior for duplicate IDs and absent-ID handling. Validate every variant; do not assume the SIMD probe or its small observed lead is portable or repeatable.",
            "C": "Explore reducing repeated neighbor-text work: parse each node’s neighbor IDs once, resolve them through an ID index, and precompute the selected successor while preserving the original scan order, strict comparison, and zero initial maximum. Measure serial and parallel successor selection and tune any threshold/schedule for this four-core target. The 5.793 ms C variant is a useful comparison, but the frontier-scatter experiment at 15.074 ms was slower than baseline; avoid assuming topological-layer parallelism helps.",
            "G": "Begin with B’s fastest hash-index variant as the integration candidate, not an assumed final winner. Test compatible A memoization and C preprocessing/scheduling changes as separate overlays and combinations; measure each complete program because gains may interact or regress. Keep the fastest variant that passes the full correctness checks. In particular preserve first-match ID lookup, neighbor tie/order behavior, float accumulation order, query ordering/text, and output formatting; discard changes that fail correctness or measured performance.",
            "analysis": "This is the DAG graph-search problem in src/graph.c. The clear measured opportunity is replacing repeated linear ID scans: baseline was correct at 11.609 ms, while the two B hash-index programs measured 5.660 and 5.679 ms. A’s contiguous-ID and neighbor-buffer changes measured 9.276 and 9.290 ms, so those layout changes alone did not beat baseline. C’s 5.793 ms result is promising but represents a broader approach; the frontier-scatter result at 15.074 ms regressed. These are individual-run measurements, not additive gains or evidence for unmeasured combinations. Keep every change general and validate against the sequential behavior."
          },
