        "synthesis": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "synthesis",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Research a faster, correctness-preserving column sort. The source uses bubble sort, which remains quadratic after the measured linear mode scan. Compare a stable O(n log n) sort for columns without NaNs against the existing path, retaining the original comparison and tie behavior; use a safe fallback where needed and keep a small-workload path if measurements justify it. The row-tiled input variant was slower (13.164 ms), so do not assume extra buffering helps.",
            "B": "Build on the best measured variant (8.317 ms): retain the sorted-run mode fast path and investigate a subquadratic fallback for columns containing NaNs, where the current implementation retains pairwise comparisons. Preserve equality semantics, earliest-in-order tie selection, float count/mode behavior, and a safe fallback if auxiliary storage fails. Include signed zero and non-finite values in correctness checks; do not keep a faster path that changes printed results.",
            "C": "Investigate whether parallel work across independent columns can help on the four-core ARM64 target, with workload-based serial/parallel selection and careful measurement of scheduling overhead. Existing OpenMP sorting (10.768 ms) and fused parallel processing (12.254 ms) were slower than the 9.395 ms baseline; do not reuse their thresholds without evidence or assume fusion and parallelism are additive.",
            "G": "Integrate tree outputs incrementally, not by blindly combining edits to the same source file. The best listed result is the mode optimization at 8.317 ms; the measured fused-sum variant is 8.979 ms, while the OpenMP and tiled-input variants were slower. Verify each integrated candidate against the full correctness checks and benchmark the complete program on the target; retain only demonstrated improvements, since combined performance may be worse than either input.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source and statement describe Problem B: seven per-column metrics, sorted columns for median and mode, and one-decimal output. The baseline measured 9.395 ms and was correct on its recorded cases. The strongest measured candidate replaces quadratic pairwise mode work with a sorted-run scan (8.317 ms); accumulating mean and harmonic-mean sums during input also improved time (8.979 ms). Other measured changes regressed, including OpenMP sorting, fused parallel processing, and row-tiled input. Objectives therefore target the remaining sort cost, correctness-sensitive mode edge cases, and cautious parallel evaluation. Every candidate must preserve results for general valid inputs, not just the measured cases."
