          "plan": {
            "A": "Explore a conservative OpenMP baseline: parallelize the outer body loop in force calculation with static scheduling, and parallelize the independent position-update loop. Keep each body's inner j loop serial and in increasing order, with a barrier before position updates. Preserve the original arithmetic and verify output correctness; measure the complete program, not just the kernel.",
            "B": "Explore keeping one OpenMP team alive across all 10 simulation steps to reduce repeated fork/join overhead. Use worksharing for the force and position-update loops, retaining the necessary barriers between them and the original serial j order and arithmetic for each body. Compare end-to-end timing and correctness against the baseline and A's candidate.",
            "C": "Explore ARM64 SIMD batching of inner-loop pair calculations, while retaining each body's contributions and accumulation in the original increasing-j order. Preserve the original float/double conversions and operation order, including the double-precision sqrt call; do not use reassociation or approximate math. Verify correctness and measure end-to-end performance; reject the approach if exact semantics cannot be maintained or it does not improve runtime.",
            "G": "When candidates arrive, assess each independently against the valid baseline, then test any proposed combination as a complete program rather than assuming gains add. Require correctness on the available suite and review that the implementation remains generic and preserves the reference computation. Compare repeated end-to-end measurements with the baseline mean of 0.66614 seconds; select the fastest valid measured candidate, or retain the baseline if none is better. No child results or interaction measurements are available yet.",
            "analysis": "The run is the N-body problem on a 4-core ARM64 target with no GPUs. The baseline is valid on 4 cases, with three measured times averaging 0.66614 seconds and score 0.5. The dominant work is 10 iterations of an all-pairs force calculation. Parallelizing independent bodies offers a direct CPU opportunity; a persistent team separately targets parallel-region overhead. SIMD is a distinct exploratory path, but preserving the reference's per-body accumulation order and floating-point semantics is important for correctness. No other candidate measurements are available, so integration gains must be measured rather than inferred."
