          "plan": {
            "A": "Explore a generic serial/parallel cutover based on `nbodies`, measuring where the serial path beats the persistent OpenMP path. The 128-body threshold was measured at 0.1750 s versus 0.1723 s for the always-parallel candidate, so do not treat it as established. Preserve correctness for every input; do not hardcode test sizes or outputs.",
            "B": "Investigate strict, correctness-preserving improvements to the pairwise force kernel, especially generated code and the `sqrt`/float-conversion path. Keep each target’s source traversal in increasing `j` order. The outer `omp simd` candidate showed no speedup, and four-target SIMD tiling regressed; avoid repeating those variants. Reject approximate math or changed accumulation order unless correctness for all valid inputs is assured.",
            "C": "Build on the measured winner: one persistent OpenMP team across all 10 iterations, static worksharing over target bodies, and the original serial inner interaction order. Explore team sizing and work partitioning within the four available cores. Preserve the barriers between force calculation, position updates, and the next iteration; measure every variant and check correctness.",
            "G": "Use the persistent-team candidate (0.1723 s) as the initial integration baseline, then evaluate programs from A, B, and C individually and in combinations. Keep an integration only if its own measured result improves on the best verified candidate; gains are not additive. Run the correctness checks on every retained program and preserve generic behavior and binary I/O.",
            "analysis": "The baseline mean is 0.6661 s. The persistent OpenMP candidate measured 0.1723 s (about 3.9× faster); a version with a serial path below 128 bodies measured 0.1750 s. The compact-position and outer-SIMD candidates were essentially baseline speed (0.6654–0.6655 s), while the two four-target tiling candidates were slower (0.8449 and 0.9055 s). Prioritize the persistent parallel approach, treat the threshold as unproven, and validate any new arithmetic or integrated variant rather than assuming its gains."
