        "preparation": {
          "model_calls": 1,
          "phase": "preparation",
          "plan": {
            "A": "Implement and benchmark an exact Kitamasa/quotient-polynomial candidate for the stated recurrence. Use the relation x^N = P1*x^(N-1) + ... + PN and compute x^(H-1) modulo that relation; dot the resulting coefficients with A1..AN. Target O(N^2 log H) work instead of dense O(N^3 log H) matrix powers. Keep all arithmetic modulo 1,000,000,007 and verify indexing, especially N=1 and H=N+1, against the unchanged correctness oracle.",
            "B": "Research a generic short-horizon path: with d=H-N small, advance the recurrence directly from the supplied N base terms using a circular buffer, costing O(N*d). Measure a conservative dispatch threshold and retain a correct fallback for larger d; do not iterate for a huge horizon. Ensure the branch depends only on input dimensions/horizon and works for arbitrary valid A and P, not observed test values.",
            "C": "Explore speeding up the existing dense-matrix candidate as an independent fallback: transpose or tile the right operand for locality, and reduce modulo in safe bounded chunks rather than after every product (for residues below the modulus, at most eight products fit in uint64_t before reduction). Measure each change separately; consider parallelizing independent output tiles only if supported by the existing build setup and worthwhile on the 4-core ARM64 target. Preserve exact modular results and avoid unsafe accumulators.",
            "G": "Integrate only candidates produced by A/B/C that pass the unchanged correctness checks. The baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.0214003 s across three runs and score 0.5; treat that as the measured baseline, not proof that the cases represent all valid inputs. Benchmark the complete integrated program, including any hybrid dispatch, rather than adding candidate speedups arithmetically. Keep a branch only if its full-program measurements justify it; preserve general-input correctness and do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
            "analysis": "The supplied source exponentiates dense N-by-N matrices with cubic multiplication and a modulo in the inner product, while the recurrence admits polynomial powering with quadratic work per exponent bit. A addresses that main algorithmic cost; B targets inputs close to the base horizon; C investigates a separate dense-kernel fallback. The observed baseline is correct but slow relative to its reference (score 0.5), and its four checked cases do not establish behavior across the full input range. Because optimizations can interact, G must measure the actual integrated candidate."
          },
